  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current approach uses SLSQP optimization with random starting points to minimize the maximum overlap integral for a given grid size (280 points). However, this method tends to get stuck in local optima and converges to similar solutions within a reasonable time frame. The proposed approach introduces multiple grid resolutions (80/280 points) with parent-child interpolation to escape local minima and explore broader solution spaces. We'll use:\n- Coarse grid optimization (80 points) to discover new global directions\n- Fine grid optimization (280 points) for higher accuracy\n- Efficient transfer between resolutions using interpolation\n- Modified objective function with regularization\nKey improvements: multi-resolution transfer, lower max iteration counts, rotated parents, and different grid sizes.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: Piecewise constant functions on [0,2]\n- Objective: C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n  - Computed as: max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * dx\n- Constraints:\n  - Sum(h) = n_points/2 (implicit in integral constraint)\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- Process:\n  1. Coarse grid (n=80): simplex-based global search\n  2. Fine grid (n=280): local refinement from best coarse candidate + initial provided parent\n  3. Interpolation between resolutions\n  4. Birthday problem (slepian-like) for additional perturbations\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n<provide complete implementations>\n- Run parameters:\n  ```\n  n_fine = 280\n  n_coarse = 80\n  dx_fine = 2.0 / n_fine\n  dx_coarse = 2.0 / n_coarse\n  total_time = 100  # seconds\n  ```\n- Coarse grid optimization (first pass):\n  ```\n  def objective_coarse(h): # Includes regularization\n  def constraint_coarse(h):\n  simplex = ... # Using Simulated Annealing instead of SLSQP\n  ```\n- Fine grid transfer/optimization (second pass):\n  ```\n  if coarse solution found:\n      h_fine = np.interp(fine_grid, coarse_grid, coarse_solution)\n  ```\n- Perturbation generation:\n  ```\n  child = np.random.rand(n_points) * 2 - 1  # Bipolar pattern\n  child = child.clip(0,1)\n  child *= (n_points/2) / np.sum(child)\n  ```\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Starting points:\n  1. Provided initial_h_values (if available)\n  2. Coarse grid \u2192 Fine grid interpolation\n  3. Birthday problem children (4 instances per grid)\n- Time distribution:\n  - 60% to coarse grid (120 optimization steps)\n  - 40% to fine grid (80 optimization steps)\n- Objective modification:\n  ```\n  def objective_robust(h):\n      corr = np.correlate(...)\n      max_corr = np.max(corr)\n      # Add regularization\n      robust_corr = max_corr - 0.0001 * np.std(corr)\n      return robust_corr * dx\n  ```\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Timing estimates:\n  - Coarse grid: ~0.5 seconds per optimization\n  - Fine grid: ~1.0 seconds per optimization\n  - Total cost: ~N*log(N) per point\n  - Budget restart: Monitor evaluation time + time_per_point\n- Special cases:\n  - Handle integer n_points constraint during interpolation\n  - Use median-based interpolation for robustness\n  - Implement fallback for finite differences\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- End checks:\n  ```\n  assert all(0 <= x <= 1 for x in h_fine)\n  integral = sum(h_fine) * dx\n  assert abs(integral - 140) < 1e-4\n  correlation = np.correlate(h_fine, 1-h_fine, mode='full')\n  best_overlap = np.max(correlation) * dx\n  assert best_overlap < 0.385 and best_overlap > 0.38\n  ```\n- Return structure:\n  ```\n  return (h_values, best_overlap_bound, n_points)\n  ```\n- Performance metrics:\n  - Requires <5 evaluations per grid\n  - Grid transfer factor: (n_fine/n_coarse) = 3.5",
